  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ARM: Vehicle-Based Mobile Laboratory to Enhance Air Quality Monitoring</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Air pollution in Armenia, particularly in Yerevan, is a significant concern  for Government, citizens and environmental groups. Recognizing this, the Environment Agency Austria, within the UNDP-implemented EU4Climate project, conducted a needs assessment of Armenia&#x2019;s ambient air monitoring system and provided recommendations for improvement. 
These recommendations became a discussion topic at the high-level policy dialogue &#x201c;GREEEN Platform&#x201d; meeting, chaired by the Deputy Prime Minister Khachatryan on 16 June 2023, following which, the Government allocated funds for two new air quality monitoring stations in Yerevan. Additionally, the Government of Japan committed to equip three additional observation stations for automatic measuring atmospheric air pollutants. These stations will enable the measurement of key air pollutants, including particular matters such as dust particles (PM2.5 and PM10), nitrogen and sulfur dioxide, carbon monoxide and ground-level ozone. It will also allow real-time data collection, which will be made publicly available and facilitate the calibration of widely used air quality sensors, including those installed at construction sites.
The &#x201c;Hydrometeorology and Monitoring Center&#x201d; state non-commercial organization (HMC), is mandated to systematically monitor environmental conditions, including air quality. Given the ongoing efforts for expansion and modernization of Armenia&apos;s atmospheric air quality monitoring system, ensuring the continuous and accurate operation of these monitoring stations is crucial. This requires constant control, diagnostics, calibration and maintenance that are essential for ensuring the accuracy of air quality data. This can be ensured through mobile services. 
A vehicle-based mobile laboratory will allow these processes to be carried out directly at the monitoring stations, preventing data gaps and increasing the reliability of the air quality measurements.</narrative>
  </description>
